# Accepted risk: openssl 3.5.8-1.amzn2023.0.1 (fix: .0.3) in public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14,
# owned by package `openssl-snapsafe-libs` / `openssl-fips-provider-latest`.
#
# UNLIKE the libxml2 entries above, this library IS genuinely loaded and used:
# `python3 -c "import ssl; print(ssl.OPENSSL_VERSION)"` reports this exact
# version, and every outbound HTTPS call this app makes (via httpx, to the
# ~20 hardcoded cantonal WMS/ESRI geoservice domains defined in
# cantons_configuration/data/*.yaml) goes through it. As of 2026-10-19,
# `dnf upgrade -y --refresh` (already run in Dockerfile.lambda) still reports
# "Nothing to do" — AWS has not yet published the patched RPM. We cannot fix
# this ourselves; re-verify with `dnf upgrade --refresh` on each dependency
# update and remove these entries once the patched version is actually
# installed.
#
# Each CVE below is suppressed because the specific vulnerable FEATURE PATH is
# never exercised by this app's TLS client usage, not because the library as a
# whole is unreachable:
#
# - QUIC-stack bugs (75804, 84784, 54873, 42772, 35191): require OpenSSL's
#   native QUIC/HTTP-3 implementation. httpcore 1.0.9 (verified installed)
#   implements only HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 over TCP+TLS — there is no QUIC
#   transport anywhere in httpx/httpcore to invoke this code.
#
# - DTLS bugs (84782, 75806): DTLS runs over UDP; httpx/httpcore only ever
#   open TCP connections. This code is linked into libssl but never called.
#
# - CMP client bug (75805): Certificate Management Protocol (RFC 4210) is an
#   unrelated OpenSSL feature for CA enrollment. Nothing in this app or its
#   dependencies performs CMP operations.
#
# - SSL_set_SSL_CTX mid-handshake swap (72897): a server-side SNI pattern
#   (swapping cert context based on ClientHello). This app runs no TLS server;
#   outbound `ssl.SSLContext` objects are created once per client connection
#   and never swapped mid-handshake.
#
# - EC/SM2 timing side-channel (54872): requires the local process to *sign*
#   with SM2 or non-NIST curves lacking constant-time code. This app only does
#   outbound TLS client handshakes (standard ECDHE on NIST curves); it never
#   generates ECDSA/SM2 signatures.
#
# - CRLDP memory DoS (35189): THE ONE CASE WITH A REAL, NARROW PATH. A
#   malicious TLS server could serve a certificate with many CRL distribution
#   points to trigger excessive heap allocation. Mitigating context: we only
#   connect outbound to a small, hardcoded set of ~20 Swiss cantonal
#   government WMS/ESRI endpoints (not user-controlled input), each call is
#   bounded by a 10-20s httpx timeout, and Lambda invocations are short-lived
#   and recycled. Worst case is a transient memory/latency hit on one
#   invocation if a trusted government endpoint were itself compromised to
#   serve a malicious cert — not data exposure, not code execution. Accepted
#   given the narrow blast radius, pending AWS's patched RPM.
CVE-2026-75804  # openssl — QUIC stream flow control not enforced, CWE-770 — no QUIC transport in httpx/httpcore
CVE-2026-84784  # openssl — QUIC unbounded RETIRE_CONNECTION_ID backlog, CWE-770 — no QUIC transport in httpx/httpcore
CVE-2026-54873  # openssl — QUIC STREAM fragment metadata DoS, CWE-770 — no QUIC transport in httpx/httpcore
CVE-2026-42772  # openssl — QUIC O(n^2) fragment reassembly CPU DoS, CWE-407 — no QUIC transport in httpx/httpcore
CVE-2026-35191  # openssl — QUIC unvalidated amplification credit (low) — no QUIC transport in httpx/httpcore
CVE-2026-84782  # openssl — DTLS stale retransmission buffer offset, CWE-125 — app never uses DTLS (UDP), httpx is TCP-only
CVE-2026-75806  # openssl — DTLS 1.2 undersized AEAD record DoS, CWE-1284 — app never uses DTLS (UDP), httpx is TCP-only
CVE-2026-75805  # openssl — CMP client NULL deref on revocation response, CWE-476 — app never performs CMP operations
CVE-2026-72897  # openssl — out-of-bounds access after SSL_set_SSL_CTX() mid-handshake, CWE-787 — app runs no TLS server, never swaps SSL_CTX
CVE-2026-54872  # openssl — timing side-channel in non-NIST EC/SM2 scalar multiplication, CWE-208 — app never signs with SM2/non-NIST curves
CVE-2026-35189  # openssl — excessive heap allocation in relative CRLDP processing, CWE-770 — narrow risk, see note above; only ~20 trusted gov endpoints contacted

